Native VU1: blocks chain to their successor (phase 3/5 attempt), lane masks in the dispatch slot, replay workbench.
- Every generated block (tools/vu1/vu1n_gen.py) ends with VU1NativeAccess::chain(n, next): runNative's bookkeeping after
  the block (timing history, FDIV/EFU commit, counters, pc) and, when the successor needs nothing runNative handles itself
  (miss, entry lane check, budget, stop, verify bisection), its entry bookkeeping too; the block then jumps to it with a
  guaranteed tail call ([[clang::musttail]]). Self-loops jump to themselves. The order of every state change is the same
  as in the dispatch loop; a block returns next | kVu1nDone when it does not chain. MSVC has no guaranteed tail call (the
  jump becomes a nested call: 13 % slower), so chaining is on with clang only; GOW_VU1N_CHAIN=0/1 overrides.
- The clean-operand masks of patch 10 are copied into the dispatch slot (no pointer to a table in the generated code).
- Diagnostics: GOW_VU1N_DIAG prints selfBlocks / selfPairs (blocks that branched back to themselves: 11 % of the block
  executions, 28 % of the pairs in ship combat). GOW_VU1N_REPLAY_REPS: the warm repetitions skip the result hash, so a
  profile of the replay shows the VU1 work.
Measured: 4000 recorded combat programs replayed (GOW_VU1N_RECORD / REPLAY), bit-identical; median of 4 alternating runs
1256 ms (before) -> 1231 ms (-2 %, noise +-3 %); in-game combat VU1 worker 3627 -> 3593 ms busy per 5 s;
GOW_VU1N_VERIFY 2000 runs, 0 mismatches. A small gain: the VU1 time is in the blocks' arithmetic (serial MADD chains on
ACC), not in the dispatch (the sampling profiler's ~40 % there was an artefact of thread-suspension sampling).

